use_collision = true
size = Vector3(0.9, 1.1, 10.4)
material = SubResource("mat_building")

; Standing on the beam, which is the only thing left at the entrance that spans.
;
; **Down from 14.1 to 8.51 on 2026-08-14c, into the wall it belongs on.** It used
; to stand clear above the roofline on the argument that that is what makes it
; readable from the ring, where the decision to walk west gets made. What it
; actually did was leave 5.6m of blank white wall directly above a doorway — with
; the board floating past the parapet against the sky above all of it.
;
; The readability argument does not survive being measured. From the ring's west
; edge the arch face is 14.5m away, so at 14.1 the board sat 36 to 45 degrees up:
; you had to crane at it. Centred in the wall band it sat at 19 to 31, which is
; where a sign over a door belongs and is *easier* to read, not harder.
;
; **That band is kept, and the wall under it is not.** Taking the top off took
; away the masonry the board was centred in, so it sits on the beam now: 7.20 to
; 10.60, lapping 0.2 into the beam so the two overlap rather than meet. From the
; ring that is 20.8 to 31.5 degrees — the same place on the eye, reached by
; standing on something instead of by being flush with something.
;
; Thinner than it was, 0.45 against 0.8, and 0.425 of that stands proud into the
; plaza. A board bolted to a wall can be as thick as it likes because no edge of
; it shows; a board standing above a beam against open sky is seen end-on from
; the ring walkway and reads as a slab at 0.8.
;
; 9.6 long inside a 10.4 beam, so the beam shows past it at both ends. A sign as
; long as the thing carrying it reads as the thing carrying it.
[node name="arch_sign" type="CSGBox3D" parent="."]
